 ### Cylindrical SDF Primitives
-A technique for rendering implicit geometry defined by SDFs using ray marching, specifically for cap cylinder shapes, often involving nested cylinder calculations for complex lighting or structural details [S1, unofficial].
+A technique for rendering implicit geometry defined by SDFs using ray marching, specifically for cap cylinder shapes [S1, unofficial]. The `capCylinder` function computes the distance as $d = \text{abs}(\text{vec2f}(\text{length}(p.yz), p.x)) - \text{vec2}(r, h)$, returning $\min(\max(d.x, d.y), 0.) + \text{length}(\max(d, \text{vec2f}(0.)))$ [S1, unofficial]. Nested cylinder calculations are used for complex lighting or structural details, such as calculating outer and inner cylinder distances and returning a boolean-like SDF via $\max(-d_1, d_2)$ [S1, unofficial]. Normals are calculated using finite differences with $h=0.001$ and kernel $k=\text{vec2f}(1, -1)$ [S1, unofficial]. Ray marching initializes $d = \text{map}(pp)$, $t = \text{abs}(d)$, and loops while counter < 30 and $d > 0.001$, updating $d = \text{map}(pp + t \times \text{rayReflect})$ and $t += \text{abs}(d)$, stepping $pp$ if $d \le 0.01$ [S1, unofficial]. Parameters include `OUTER_RADIUS` (0.4255), `INNER_RADIUS` (0.385), and `LIGHT_POS` (0.035) [S1, unofficial].
 
